Subject: Add the AOT deoptimiser and the guard sweep

What this does
  `qjs_aot_deopt` leaves compiled code: it records the pc to resume at and
  returns, and the AOT entry in `js_regvm_execute` starts the **threaded**
  interpreter at that pc with the same record and register file, so the rest of
  the activation runs the way it would have without compiled code. It never
  re-enters compiled code and never runs the step engine.

  Under `--deopt-everywhere` every instruction becomes a deopt site and a guard
  miss deopts instead of rejoining; `QJS_AOT_SWEEP=<site>:<n>` then forces
  guard <site> to fail on its <n>-th execution regardless of its condition
  ("all:<n>" sweeps every site, "off" disables the sweep), so a per-site sweep
  exercises every frame state and compares the result with the interpreter.

Why we need it
  A guard that fails must not branch to a slow path that rejoins compiled code:
  that is what forces state into memory at every op boundary and makes a
  re-entering unit slower than the interpreter. A deopt needs nothing built,
  because a compiled frame and an interpreter frame share the same register
  array, but it must continue on the interpreter the shipping build uses, and
  its guards must be testable one by one.

What it adds
  `qjs_aot_deopt`, `qjs_aot_should_deopt`, `QJS_AOT_NO_DEOPT`, `JSRegRun.deopt_pc`
  in the engine; the generator's `--deopt-everywhere` and the `QJS_AOT_SWEEP`
  sweep. `QJS_AOT_SWEEP` reports its coverage in the `QJS_AOT_STATS` line.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.

